## 2. Complete original record law

Work on the full physical rotor P space at a fixed finite graph: every A site is occupied, all allowed B occupations and electric words remain, and Gauss law is retained. Write

    h = K D + delta H4,    H4 = -2 sum_(a<c) S_ac* S_ac,
    J_mu = sqrt(kappa) B_mu,    R_mu = J_mu* J_mu,
    Gamma = sum_mu R_mu,    F = f(N).

The actual D includes its occupied-B vacancy gates. D is nonnegative multiplication, h is self-adjoint on D(D), and H4 is bounded at fixed graph. Number projections commute strongly with h. Each original resolved formation amplitude increases total occupied-site number by exactly two; hence [N,J_mu]=2 J_mu and [N,R_mu]=0.

The root opens with resolved jumps. Its proof also applies to the actual coherent channels, but this should be said: a prescribed coherent sum of the resolved sign amplitudes still satisfies [N,J_coh]=2 J_coh. The amplitude sum, including its prescribed normalization, remains intact. Replacing it by a sum of dissipators would change the instrument and is neither needed nor justified.

The initial density and original instrument are held fixed across the comparison family; an additional preparation rule that changes the input with f is not covered by this equality. N has finite spectrum even though rotor fields are unbounded. Thus every real f on that spectrum gives a bounded self-adjoint F commuting with h and Gamma. The no-event contraction factorization

    V_f(u) = exp(-iuF) V_0(u),
    V_0(u) = exp[u(-ih-Gamma/2)]

is valid without assuming h commutes with Gamma. For a history r containing k marks at t_1,...,t_k, with t_0=0 and t_(k+1)=T, its operator obeys

    A_f(r) P_n = exp[-i Phi_r(n)] A_0(r) P_n,
    Phi_r(n) = sum_(a=0)^k f(n+2a)(t_(a+1)-t_a).

The final no-event segment includes absence of a further mark up to T. Impossible source numbers annihilate the entire history, so no value of f outside the physical number spectrum affects a nonzero amplitude. Since A_0(r) has number grade 2k, the history effect A_f(r)*A_f(r) equals A_0(r)*A_0(r), as a bounded operator. Therefore every normal density has the same marked-history probability density, including densities with inter-number coherences.

The root's bounded-Gamma trajectory expansion is sufficient. PRE additionally observes that strictly raising N on a finite spectrum gives a finite maximum number of marks, so the expansion actually terminates after finitely many mark slots. In either construction, integration over time simplices and sums over marks give the same complete finite-horizon classical path law. This covers arbitrary measurable bins, censored waiting times, coarse records, and statistics allowing other earlier, intervening, or later original formations. No small-time or weak-field approximation enters. Simultaneous jumps and prescribed isolated jump times have zero measure under the bounded-rate continuous-time law; the no-event atom is retained.

For a density commuting with N, every full conditional state agrees between the models, as does the unconditional density. For a general density, only number-diagonal blocks are guaranteed identical. On a specified path, its n,m input block acquires exp[-i(Phi_r(n)-Phi_r(m))]. Thus all fixed bounded number-commuting observables agree, including after conditioning on any positive-probability classical record event. Full states, and probes that convert inter-number phases into populations, need not agree. For nonlinear f, summing histories generally does not reduce the state change to one universal conjugation by exp[-iTf(N)]; this distinction was already derived and controlled in PRE.

For additional interventions, the safe precise extension is to Kraus operators K_alpha satisfying [K_alpha,N]=0, including their recorded outcomes. They can be inserted in the same history argument with grade zero. Merely requiring their effects sum_alpha K_alpha* K_alpha to commute with N does not constrain state updates enough: an arbitrary unitary has effect I and can nevertheless mix number sectors. The root's informal phrase about commuting operations is read with the number-preserving Kraus condition, not as invariance under arbitrary quantum testers.

## 3. Energy expressions and a sufficient domain

The comparison h_f=h+F is a family of different supplied Hamiltonians. Its bounded perturbation shares the self-adjoint domain of h. A constant f is an ordinary scalar energy zero. Nonconstant f changes number-sector gaps, even though the stipulated count law cannot detect it.

Where the base forms exist, the selected dissipative power is

    Pi_j(h;rho) = Tr rho [J_j* h J_j - {R_j,h}/2].

The exact shift is

    Pi_j(h+F;rho) - Pi_j(h;rho)
      = Tr rho R_j Delta_2 f(N),
    Delta_2 f(n) = f(n+2)-f(n).

R_j annihilates any source sector for which the shifted number is impossible. The right side is bounded and real for every normal density. It does not make an undefined base power finite, nor justify subtracting infinite quantities. For f(n)=gamma n it is 2 gamma r_j, where r_j=Tr rho R_j. At any fixed parameters and state with r_j>0, this one real power can take any prescribed finite value by choosing gamma. That statement neither independently adjusts every channel nor constructs an energy-conserving source or reservoir.

The root's phrase “input in the common energy domain” is adequate when it explicitly means vectors in the operator domain with a valid gain/loss form, but could be misread as merely a finite first energy mean. The following direct sufficient condition removes that ambiguity. This domain argument is a POST contribution, not part of the sealed author derivation or the earlier blind PRE proof.

Orient edges A to B. In the physical P basis,

    D(q,E) = sum_(a in A, b~a, q_b=0) E_ab(E_ab-q_a).

For integer E and q_a=+1 or -1 each summand is nonnegative. Fix one primitive nonzero summand T of a resolved birth at anchor a: the old charge eta=q_a hops to a vacant B destination d, and creation fills the now-vacant a and another vacant B site b. These destinations are distinct on a legal word. The output A charge is sigma and the two previously vacant B sites are occupied. All terms of D incident on b or d consequently disappear. The only other possible change is q_a from eta to sigma on the remaining vacant neighbours of a; their electric fields have not changed. For a sign flip, setting x=eta E gives the exact inequality

    x(x+1) <= 3x(x-1)+2,
    3x(x-1)+2-x(x+1) = 2(x-1)^2 >= 0.

Without a flip the contribution is unchanged. Removing nonnegative terms at the filled B sites therefore gives, for each allowed primitive output,

    D(output) <= 3 D(input) + 2 deg(a).

This uses the actual gates and charges; it would not follow from treating occupied-B edges as an empty-B electric quadratic form. A fixed primitive destination/sign hop is a partial basis isometry: the deposited old charge and the shifted link values determine its inverse on its range. Hence, first on finite words and then by graph-norm completion,

    ||D T psi|| <= 3 ||D psi|| + 2 deg(a) ||psi||.

Each actual resolved or coherent J_j is a finite linear combination of these maps with fixed coefficients. Thus J_j maps D(D) into D(D), with a finite graph-norm bound. Since h=KD plus a bounded term and K>0, this is also preservation of D(h)=D(h+F).

For every pure psi in D(h), define the power as the finite quadratic form

    p_j(h;psi) = <J_j psi,h J_j psi>
                 - Re <J_j psi,J_j h psi>.

It equals the displayed gain-minus-anticommutator expression wherever all its strong products exist, and provides the needed form interpretation without requiring h R_j psi to exist. No claim that J_j* or R_j preserves D(h) is being made: undoing a birth can reopen a previously ungated electric direction. The mixed-state condition Tr(h^2 rho)<infinity is a direct sufficient assumption. Its spectral decomposition lies in D(h) with square-summable graph norms, so both gain and loss forms are absolutely summable by the J_j graph bound and Cauchy-Schwarz. Bounded F preserves this condition, and the offset formula extends by these sums.

This is a sufficient condition, not a claimed optimal domain. A more general state is covered whenever its base gain/loss forms are well defined and absolutely integrable. No general differentiability or integrated balance for unbounded full h is inferred only from trace-norm state evolution. The bounded offset contribution, including the dual-generator identity for F, needs no electric-energy moment.

The full conditional or evolved h-energy is compared using its number-diagonal form blocks. Whenever the needed moments exist, unchanged number-diagonal states imply

    <h+F>_f = <h>_0 + <F>_0.

For two normalized preparations in the same definite N=n sector, their input mean-energy difference is unchanged. The root's reference to a charged vacuum/reference-excitation pair is only this algebraic application; its separate preparation and energy calculation are not rechecked here. Same-number selected output mean differences likewise cancel a common f(n+2). PRE additionally derived the variance formula, nonlinear conditional rotations, and the bounded accumulated offset ledger; none is attributed to the root generation.

